<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">
        <!-- Three developer-only reminders in one sweep
             (models/kpi_work_session.py _cron_check_long_breaks):

             1. The break / lunch / meeting open RIGHT NOW has run past its
                limit. Break repeats every break_alert_minutes, capped at
                BREAK_ALERT_MAX (4) so a genuinely long break is never nagged
                forever; lunch and meeting are sent ONCE.
             2. Break + lunch TOTAL for the day has passed break_budget_minutes.
                This is what catches six short breaks, none of which was ever
                long enough to trip (1).
             3. The workday is still open past standard_workday_hours of
                presence — sent BEFORE _cron_close_overdue_workdays closes the
                day for them and stamps auto_closed.

             Every one of these goes to the DEVELOPER and to nobody else. The
             rule is written on kpi.nontask.block.reason and it holds here:
             telling an admin about someone's lunch is the false alarm that
             makes the whole feed worth ignoring. Admins can already see a long
             break live on /kpi_owner/live_tracking if they go looking.

             Unlike the idle sweep, NOTHING arms these — no ..._at field to set
             on the way in. Each check derives its own timing from the interval
             or total it is about, which is why start_break / switch_break /
             _set_nontask_reason / pause / pause_all / idle_reason all needed no
             change: six call sites that would each have had to remember to arm
             a timer, and any one of them could have been missed.

             ONE MINUTE, not five. Here the interval IS close to the resolution
             of the thing being measured: on a 5-minute sweep a "30 minute"
             lunch reminder lands anywhere up to 35 minutes, and that is not a
             30-minute reminder. The sweep itself is cheap — one search over
             open sessions, of which there are as many as there are people
             currently at work.

             Every threshold lives on res.company and is editable in the app's
             Configuration screen; 0 switches that one off. -->
        <record id="ir_cron_kpi_break_alert" model="ir.cron">
            <field name="name">KRA/KPI: Remind developers about long breaks and open workdays</field>
            <field name="model_id" ref="model_kpi_work_session"/>
            <field name="state">code</field>
            <field name="code">model._cron_check_long_breaks()</field>
            <field name="interval_number">1</field>
            <field name="interval_type">minutes</field>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
        </record>
    </data>
</odoo>
